n\nIn a math class of 30 students, 12 out of 15 girls are freshmen and 11 out of 15 boys are freshmen. What is the probability that in a randomly selected group of five students from the class, there will be two freshmen girls and three freshmen boys? Express your answer as a decimal to the nearest thousandth.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"188998196b5431a77effc4882267520ee4585797560bc025092b4f7cfd36383b"},"seconds":15.941334962844849,"seed":16780794370814389,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we need to find the probability that in a randomly selected group of five students from the class, there will be two freshmen girls and three freshmen boys. We will use the hypergeometric distribution for this calculation.\n\nFirst, let's determine the number of freshmen girls and boys in the class:\n- There are 15 girls in total, and 12 of them are freshmen. So, there are 12 freshmen girls.\n- There are 15 boys in total, and 11 of them are freshmen. So, there are 11 freshmen boys.\n- The total number of students is 30, which is the sum of 15 girls and 15 boys.\n\nWe need to find the probability of selecting 2 freshmen girls out of 12 and 3 freshmen boys out of 11 in a group of 5 students selected from the class. The total number of ways to choose 5 students from 30 is given by the combination formula \\(\\binom{30}{5}\\).\n\nThe number of ways to choose 2 freshmen girls from 12 is \\(\\binom{12}{2}\\), and the number of ways to choose 3 freshmen boys from 11 is \\(\\binom{11}{3}\\). The number of ways to choose the remaining 0 students from the remaining 17 students (30 - 12 - 11) is \\(\\binom{17}{0}\\), which is 1.\n\nThe probability is then given by the ratio of the number of favorable outcomes to the total number of outcomes:\n\\[\nP = \\frac{\\binom{12}{2} \\binom{11}{3} \\binom{17}{0}}{\\binom{30}{5}}\n\\]\n\nCalculating each combination:\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{2} = \\frac{12 \\times 11}{2 \\times 1} = 66\n\\]\n\\[\n\\binom{11}{3} = \\frac{11 \\times 10 \\times 9}{3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = 165\n\\]\n\\[\n\\binom{17}{0} = 1\n\\]\n\\[\n\\binom{30}{5} = \\frac{30 \\times 29 \\times 28 \\times 27 \\times 26}{5 \\times 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = 142506\n\\]\n\nSo, the probability is:\n\\[\nP = \\frac{66 \\times 165 \\times 1}{142506} = \\frac{10890}{142506} \\approx 0.0764\n\\]\n\nRounding to the nearest thousandth, we get:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0.076}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{0.076}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"counting_and_probability:111"}
